    6 => [
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Financial Records and Farm Accounting',
                'summary' => 'Simple accounting that keeps the farm solvent.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "A farm manager does not need an accountant's qualification, but does need an accountant's discipline. Simple, current financial records are the difference between knowing your position and guessing it.\n\nStart with two books: an income record and an expense record. Every sale, every purchase, every payment to a worker goes into one of them, dated and described. The habit of recording on the day of the transaction is what makes the books trustworthy; a month of reconstruction is full of errors.\n\nFrom those two books, produce a monthly summary: total income, total expense, and the difference. That single number, month over month, is the farm's pulse. A manager who watches the pulse catches a cash squeeze while there is still time to adjust, rather than at harvest when it is too late.\n\nTrack cost per hectare and cost per nut produced. These two ratios tell you whether a change in practice actually improved profitability. Many farms increase activity without increasing profit, and only the unit-cost numbers reveal the difference between busyness and progress.\n\nFinally, separate farm money from personal money, even if it is two wallets or two bank accounts. Mixed money makes the records useless and the tax season painful. The manager who keeps clean books runs the farm on evidence, and evidence is what lenders, buyers, and programs all ask for.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Mechanisation and Equipment Management',
                'summary' => 'Choose, maintain, and manage farm equipment well.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "Equipment, from a manual sprayer to a tractor, multiplies a farm's reach. Managing it well means choosing the right tool, keeping it running, and knowing when the investment pays back.\n\nChoose equipment against the work, not the catalogue. A small farm does not need a large tractor, and a large farm cannot run on hand tools alone. Match the machine to the hectares, the terrain, and the tasks that actually consume the most labour. The right-sized tool pays for itself; the oversized one becomes an expensive ornament.\n\nMaintain before failure. Keep a log for each machine: service dates, repairs, and the next check. Inspect equipment before each use, because a breakdown discovered mid-task costs a day and a harvest's worth of stress. Spare parts for the failure-prone components are cheap insurance against a stopped season.\n\nCost the equipment honestly. Fuel, service, repairs, and depreciation are real costs that belong in the unit-cost figures. A tractor that looks free because it was bought once still costs money every season, and those costs belong in the price of the nuts.\n\nFinally, train every operator. A machine used incorrectly is unsafe and wears out early. One trained operator per machine, a written checklist, and a service log turn equipment from a liability into the farm's leverage.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Water Management and Irrigation',
                'summary' => 'Water the palms at the right time and rate.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Water is the input coconut palms cannot skip, and water management is the discipline of giving them enough, at the right time, without waste. In dry regions it is the single biggest driver of yield.\n\nUnderstand the palm's need. Mature palms need substantial water year-round, with critical windows during flowering and nut development. Young palms need steady moisture to establish. A palm stressed by drought drops nuts and produces smaller fruit; the cost of water is far less than the cost of lost yield.\n\nChoose the system for the farm. Drip irrigation delivers water slowly to the root zone and wastes little; basin or ring watering around each palm is the low-cost alternative. Whatever the system, the goal is deep, infrequent watering that encourages roots down, rather than shallow daily sprinkling that leaves them near the surface.\n\nSchedule by need, not by habit. Water more in hot, dry, windy weather and less during rains. Check the soil moisture a few inches down; dry soil at root depth is the signal. Overwatering in heavy clay waterlogs roots and invites rot, so drainage matters as much as supply.\n\nFinally, protect the water source. Store water where possible, keep channels and tanks clean, and prevent runoff from carrying chemicals into streams. The manager who manages water well grows through the dry season while neighbours wait for rain.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Replanting and Estate Renewal',
                'summary' => 'Renew aging palms without collapsing production.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "Replanting is the long game of coconut farming: palms live for decades, and an estate that never renews slowly declines. The skill is renewing without a production collapse.\n\nStart with a stand audit. Count palms by age class and mark the senile ones: low yields, weak crowns, damaged trunks. A farm where more than a quarter of palms are senile needs a renewal program; one with mostly mature palms needs only replacement of losses.\n\nReplant as a rolling program, not a single event. Replace a small number of senile palms each year, funded by current harvests, so total production stays level. A farm that clears a whole block at once trades a decade of income for a decade of waiting; a farm that renews a few palms a year never feels the dip.\n\nUse certified seedlings from a reputable nursery, plant at the start of the rains, and water the first dry season. Protect young palms from weeds, animals, and fire in their vulnerable first years, and intercrop for income while they mature.\n\nFinally, record the renewal. Track which blocks were replanted, when, and with what variety. The renewal record is the farm's forecast: it tells you which blocks will come into bearing, when peak production returns, and which areas need attention next. Renewal done on record is renewal done right.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Market Channels and Selling Strategy',
                'summary' => 'Choose the channels that pay the farm best.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Growing excellent nuts is only half the business; selling them well is the other half. Market channels and selling strategy decide what a farm actually earns for its harvest.\n\nKnow your channels. Local fresh markets pay quickly but at farm-gate prices; processors buy in bulk at negotiated rates; exporters pay premiums but demand grade, volume, and documentation. Each channel has its own requirements, and the best channel depends on your grade, quantity, location, and capacity to wait.\n\nGrade to the channel. Premium channels pay for premium nuts: clean, mature, undamaged, of consistent size. Sorting at harvest and storage lets you send the best nuts to the premium channel and the rest to the bulk channel, instead of selling the whole harvest at the lowest common price.\n\nTime the sale. Prices move with supply: harvest gluts depress prices, and off-season scarcity lifts them. Where you can store nuts safely, holding for a better window adds real value, but only if storage costs less than the price gain. Selling everything on day one is rarely the best price.\n\nFinally, build relationships and use the platform. Repeat buyers, the cooperative's marketplace, and verified supply programs all reward reliable sellers. The manager who sells with a strategy, rather than to whoever arrives first, earns the farm's true worth.",
            ],
        ],
