    7 => [
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Device and App Mastery',
                'summary' => 'Use the capture tools accurately and reliably.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "Your phone and the platform's app are your primary tools. Mastering them means the evidence you capture is accurate, complete, and submitted the first time.\n\nPrepare the device before you leave. Charge it fully, clear storage for photos, and confirm the app is updated and logged in. A phone that dies mid-visit or an app that crashes loses the whole visit's evidence, and a second trip costs everyone time.\n\nLearn the capture flow until it is automatic. GPS reading, photo capture, and record submission each have a correct sequence. Capture the GPS at the farm's identifiable point, hold still for a stable fix, and confirm the accuracy shown before moving on. Rushing the fix is the most common evidence defect.\n\nHandle the common failures. A weak signal needs open sky and patience; a full storage needs a quick clear; a failed submission needs a retry, not a reconstruction from memory. Know each fix before you need it, and never fabricate or guess a coordinate to save time.\n\nFinally, sync and back up the same day. Submit records while the details are fresh, and confirm the submission landed in the system. The agent whose device is always ready, and whose evidence always lands, is the agent the verification team trusts most.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Verifying Providers and Depots',
                'summary' => 'Apply the same rigor to businesses as to farms.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "Field verification is not only about farms. Input and service providers, depots, and collection points all need verification, and the rigor is the same: prove the business is real, reachable, and accurately described.\n\nStart with the business identity. Confirm the registered name, the owner or authorised representative, and their identification, and match them to the profile. A business whose documents, profile, and bank details disagree is a verification risk, not a formality.\n\nVerify the physical operation. Visit the declared base of operations: the depot, stall, warehouse, or office. Capture the GPS at the entrance and photos that show the operation, the stock or equipment, and any signage. A provider with no physical presence, or a GPS point that lands on empty ground, has failed verification.\n\nCheck the declared capability. Does the stock match the listings? Does the service capacity match the claims? Sample a listing against the shelf, and a coverage claim against the map. The sample-check is the heart of provider verification, and it protects every buyer who relies on the badge.\n\nFinally, document and report with the same standard as a farm visit: factual notes, complete photos, same-day submission. Provider verification carries the platform's trust to the marketplace, and the agent who verifies providers carefully keeps that trust intact.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Seasonal and Weather Challenges',
                'summary' => 'Verify reliably through rains, heat, and harvest.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Field work does not pause for weather, and verification quality should not drop with it. The seasons bring predictable challenges, and the prepared agent meets them with a plan.\n\nPlan around the rains. Heavy rain makes roads impassable and GPS fixes weak under cloud and canopy. Schedule visits in weather windows, allow extra travel time, and when a fix will not settle in open sky, note the reduced accuracy honestly rather than guessing. A documented limitation beats a fabricated coordinate every time.\n\nManage the heat. Hot seasons exhaust both agent and equipment. Carry water, plan the route for shade where possible, and protect the device from overheating. A heat-stressed agent makes rushed, error-prone records; the agent who manages their own condition makes better evidence.\n\nExpect harvest-season pressure. Harvest is when verification demand peaks and growers are busiest. Book visits in advance, be flexible about timing, and keep the evidence standard even when the day is long. The busiest season is when accuracy matters most, because the records drive program eligibility.\n\nFinally, adapt the route, not the standard. Weather changes how you reach the farm, never what you record. The agent who keeps the standard through every season is the one whose work survives audit.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Working with Community Leaders',
                'summary' => 'Build trust through local leadership.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "A field agent rarely works alone in a community. Local leaders, cooperative chairpersons, and elders shape whether growers welcome you or doubt you, so working with them is a core skill.\n\nIntroduce yourself to the leadership first. Before visiting farms, meet the local leader, explain who you represent and the purpose of the program, and ask for their guidance. A visit endorsed by a trusted leader is a visit that opens doors; one that arrives unannounced is one that meets suspicion.\n\nUse leaders as partners, not shortcuts. A leader can help you understand local land arrangements, identify the real farm operators, and resolve questions about shared plots. But the leader's word is a starting point, not evidence; you still verify identity, location, and facts with your own records.\n\nRespect the community's structures. Attend the meetings, follow the local courtesies, and be transparent about what the program offers and what it does not. Communities remember agents who were honest about a program's limits, and they forget those who overpromised.\n\nFinally, keep the relationship clean. Never accept gifts or payments for enrollment or verification, and never let a leader pressure you into approving a farm that does not meet the standard. The agent who is honest with leaders, and firm on standards, earns the community's respect and the platform's trust.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Quality Assurance of Your Own Work',
                'summary' => 'Review your evidence before the reviewer does.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "The best field agents review their own work before the verification team does. Self-review catches the defects that cause rejections and rework, and it is a habit you can build in minutes a day.\n\nBuild a personal checklist from the standard: identity confirmed, GPS at the identifiable point with a stable fix, complete photo set with landmarks, factual captions, declared facts checked, and same-day submission. Run it on every visit before you leave the farm, while a re-take is still possible.\n\nReview your week's records in a quiet moment. Read the notes as a reviewer would: is 'farm looks good' evidence, or is 'three dead palms in block two'? Rewrite vague notes into specific facts. The note that states the fact is the note that survives review.\n\nWatch your own patterns. Every agent has a recurring weakness: a rushed GPS, a missing caption, a forgotten document check. Notice yours and correct it deliberately. An agent who fixes their own pattern improves faster than one who only reacts to rejected visits.\n\nFinally, ask for feedback on rejected or questioned records, and apply it. The reviewer's comment is free coaching. The agent who treats feedback as a tool, not a criticism, becomes the reference standard other agents learn from.",
