    9 => [
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Supply Chain and Supplier Verification',
                'summary' => 'Know where your stock really comes from.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "An input provider is only as reliable as their supply chain. Verifying your suppliers is the discipline that keeps counterfeit and unregistered products off your shelf and protects the farmers who trust your store.\n\nStart with supplier verification. Before you stock a product, confirm the supplier is legitimate: their registration, their distribution rights, and their source for the product. Keep the supplier's details and invoices on file, because that paperwork is your proof of a genuine supply chain.\n\nCheck every delivery against the order. Confirm the product, the formulation, the quantity, and the batch numbers, and inspect the packaging and labels for damage or tampering. A delivery that does not match the order, or a product with a broken seal, is quarantined and questioned, not shelved.\n\nGuard against the grey market. Products diverted from other regions, repackaged, or sourced from unverified traders are a persistent risk in agricultural supply. The price may be tempting, but an unverified source puts your accreditation and your customers at risk. If the price seems too good, the supply chain is the first place to look.\n\nFinally, review your suppliers periodically. A supplier who was reliable last year may change their source or standards. The provider who verifies the supply chain, not just the product, is the provider whose shelf a regulator can trust.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Inventory Management and Stock Control',
                'summary' => 'Keep the right stock, in the right condition.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "Inventory is the provider's capital sitting on the shelf. Managing it well means having what customers need, in sellable condition, without money tied up in stock that ages or expires.\n\nStart with a stock record that shows, for every product: quantity on hand, batch numbers, expiry dates, and reorder level. This record is the basis of everything else, from ordering to recall response, and it only works if it is updated with every sale and delivery.\n\nRotate stock by expiry. Sell the oldest stock first, so nothing expires on the shelf, and mark down or return items approaching their date. An expired product sold to a farmer is a failed crop and a liability; a provider who rotates stock never faces that.\n\nOrder to demand, not to habit. Use the sales record to see what actually sells, and order the fast movers regularly while keeping slow movers lean. Overstocking ties up cash and risks expiry; understocking loses the sale. The reorder level, set from real sales, is the balance point.\n\nFinally, count the stock regularly. A periodic physical count against the record catches theft, damage, and error early. The provider whose records match the shelf is the provider who can promise stock honestly and deliver it reliably.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Recalls and Product Safety Response',
                'summary' => 'Act fast and correctly when a product fails.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "Every product business must be ready for the day a product is found unsafe or ineffective. A recall handled well protects customers and the business; one handled slowly damages both.\n\nPrepare before it happens. Keep your stock records with batch numbers, so if a product is recalled you can identify exactly which batches you hold and who bought them. This record is the recall's foundation, and without it you cannot scope the problem.\n\nAct immediately on a recall or safety alert. Pull the affected batches from the shelf, quarantine them, and notify the customers you can identify. Follow the regulator's or supplier's instructions exactly, and document every step: what was pulled, when, and how customers were informed.\n\nInvestigate and report. If a customer reports a product failure, record the details, the batch, and the conditions of use, and report it through the required channels. A serious report escalated early is a problem contained; a serious report ignored becomes a scandal.\n\nFinally, learn from it. After the event, review the cause: was it a supply chain failure, a storage failure, or a handling failure? Fix the cause, and record the lesson. The provider who responds fast and learns fast keeps their accreditation and their customers' trust.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Customer Education and Responsible Use',
                'summary' => 'Help farmers use products safely and effectively.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "An input provider's responsibility does not end at the sale. Educating customers on responsible use protects the crop, the farmer, the consumer, and the environment, and it is a mark of a professional provider.\n\nPoint to the label, every time. The label carries the formulation, the rate, the method, the PPE, and the pre-harvest interval. Show the customer where each of these is printed, and explain that the label is the law, not a suggestion. A customer who reads the label is a customer who uses the product correctly.\n\nProvide clear, simple guidance at the point of sale. For common products, keep a one-page guide or a printed label summary. Explain the basics: measure, don't guess; use the recommended rate; wear the PPE; observe the pre-harvest interval. Keep the guidance within what the label supports, and refer diagnosis to a certified agronomist.\n\nWarn against the common mistakes: mixing products, doubling the rate, spraying in wind or rain, and reusing containers. These mistakes are the cause of most product failures and poisonings, and a clear warning at the shelf prevents them.\n\nFinally, keep the education honest. Never claim a product does more than its label supports, and never advise beyond your qualification. The provider who educates responsibly sells less per customer but keeps them for years.",
