    10 => [
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Health, Safety and Environmental Duty',
                'summary' => 'Run a service that harms no one.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "A service business that works on farms carries a duty of care to its own people, its customers, and the land. Health, safety, and environmental responsibility is not overhead; it is the licence to operate.\n\nStart with your people. Every field worker deserves a safe job: the right protective equipment for the task, a briefing on the hazards, and the authority to stop work that is unsafe. A team that goes home healthy is a team that stays, and the cost of an injury is far higher than the cost of prevention.\n\nManage the environmental duty. Services that apply chemicals, disturb soil, or move water must do so within the rules: the labelled rate, the right weather, and no runoff into streams. A service that protects the land protects its customers' farms and its own future.\n\nPrepare for the incident. Keep a basic first-aid kit, an emergency contact list, and a spill kit appropriate to the work, and train the team on what to do. When something goes wrong, the priorities are people first, then the environment, then the record.\n\nFinally, document the safety work: briefings, incidents, near-misses, and the fixes. A service that can show its safety record wins the contracts that matter, and the provider who treats safety as a system, not a slogan, builds a business that lasts.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Contracts and Service Agreements',
                'summary' => 'Put every job in a clear agreement.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "The difference between a professional service and a handshake is a written agreement. Contracts and service agreements protect both parties and turn expectations into obligations.\n\nDefine the scope precisely. A service agreement states what will be done, to what standard, where, by when, and for what price. The more precise the scope, the fewer the disputes; 'prune fifty palms to the standard we agreed' is a contract, 'do the pruning' is an argument waiting to happen.\n\nState the terms that matter: price, payment timing, cancellation, and what happens if the work is not done to standard. A clear agreement lets you quote with confidence and lets the customer know exactly what they are buying.\n\nKeep evidence of the work. The dispatch record, the before-and-after photos, and the customer's sign-off are the proof that the agreement was honoured. When a dispute arises, the evidence settles it in minutes rather than months.\n\nFinally, learn the contract discipline of the platform. Orders confirmed on the platform, payments through the platform flow, and disputes handled by policy are the agreements that the marketplace protects. The provider who works within written agreements and the platform's rules is the provider whose growth is sustainable.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Technology for Service Operations',
                'summary' => 'Use simple tools to run the service better.',
