                'content' => "Service operations run on information: bookings, schedules, job records, and customer history. Simple technology keeps that information organised, and the provider who uses it well outperforms the one who relies on memory.\n\nStart with a job list and schedule. Even a shared phone calendar and a written daily list turn a chaotic week into a planned one. The schedule shows which jobs, where, and when, and it is the basis for routing and for promising customers a time you can keep.\n\nRecord each job digitally. A note per job, with the date, the work done, the materials used, and the customer's confirmation, builds a service history. That history is your evidence, your improvement data, and your memory for a returning customer's preferences.\n\nUse the platform's tools. Booking, messaging, and the order flow keep the customer informed and the transaction protected. A provider who responds in the platform and completes on the platform is a provider the marketplace can trust and surface.\n\nFinally, measure what the tools give you: jobs per week, travel time, repeat customers, and on-time rate. The provider who reads these numbers knows whether the service is growing or just busy, and knows exactly where to improve next.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Handling Complaints and Service Recovery',
                'summary' => 'Turn a failure into a loyal customer.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "Every service fails sometimes. What separates a great provider from an average one is not the absence of complaints but the quality of the recovery. Service recovery is a skill, and it is learnable.\n\nListen fully before you respond. Let the customer describe the problem without interruption, and confirm you understand it. A customer who feels heard is already halfway to satisfied; a customer who feels dismissed is already gone, whatever the fix.\n\nOwn the problem and fix it fast. If the work fell short, say so plainly, apologise, and offer the remedy: redo the job, refund, or a discount on the next service. A fast, owned fix converts a complaint into loyalty; a defensive argument converts a small problem into a lost customer and a bad review.\n\nRecord every complaint and its cause. Complaints are free audits of your operation. The pattern in the records, the same crew, the same step, the same weather, points at the fix that prevents the next complaint.\n\nFinally, follow up after the fix. A customer who was satisfied with the recovery often becomes more loyal than one who never had a problem. The provider who recovers well turns their failures into their strongest referrals.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Pricing Services and Quoting Work',
                'summary' => 'Quote work that pays you properly.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "Pricing a service is a skill that protects both the provider and the customer. Quote too low and the work loses money; quote too high and the work never arrives. The discipline is quoting from cost, not from guesswork.\n\nKnow your cost per job. Labour, travel, materials, equipment, and overhead, divided by the jobs you expect to do, give you the floor price. A quote below that floor is a loss, however busy it keeps you. The provider who knows their cost can quote with confidence.\n\nQuote from the job, not from the customer. Price by the work's scope: the number of palms, the area, the difficulty, the access. A written quote that itemises the work lets the customer compare providers fairly and lets you defend your price with facts.\n\nBuild in the margin and the risk. A fair margin is what lets you maintain equipment, train staff, and survive a slow season. And price the risk: hard-to-reach plots, weather windows, and difficult jobs all carry a premium, because they cost more when they go wrong.\n\nFinally, keep the quote in writing and confirm the scope before you start. The provider who quotes from cost, in writing, with a clear scope, is the provider who gets paid properly and never has to apologise for a loss-making job.",
